    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">March 1942</span><span class="rf-text">Japanese carrier raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> drive the British Eastern Fleet to Kilindini, and Churchill agrees that planning for an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> should begin</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1942</span><span class="rf-text">Operation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ironclad">Ironclad</a>: British forces land at Courrier Bay and Ambararata Bay, west of Diego-Suarez</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1942</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-marines">Royal Marines</a> landed from HMS Anthony break the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> defence at Antisarane, and Diego-Suarez falls</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1942</span><span class="rf-text">Japanese midget submarines damage HMS Ramillies and sink the tanker British Loyalty in Diego-Suarez harbour</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1942</span><span class="rf-text">Operations Stream, Line and Jane: landings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahajanga">Majunga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toamasina">Tamatave</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Tananarive</a> falls</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1942</span><span class="rf-text">King's African Rifles ambush <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> troops at Andramanalina, and 800 surrender</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1942</span><span class="rf-text">An armistice is signed at Ambalavao, and Annet surrenders two days later</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-madagascar" data-v="0299293f6a" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The landings west of Diego-Suarez</b> The first wave of the 29th Brigade landed at Ambararata Bay and the 17th Brigade at Courrier Bay. The Vichy troops were concentrated around Diego-Suarez, and the defences of Antisarane lay ahead.</li><li><b>Both brigades advance inland</b> The 17th Brigade took the town of Diego-Suarez after crossing mangrove swamp and bush. The 29th Brigade, with the tanks, advanced 21 miles towards Antisarane against light resistance.</li><li><b>A frontal assault on the line fails</b> The 29th Brigade and its tanks attacked the defences head on. Ten of the twelve tanks were destroyed by the end of the day, and the British had not known how strong the line was.</li><li><b>The South Lancashires work round the line</b> The South Lancashires went round the Vichy defences through the swamps and rough ground, and swung behind the line. They fired on the defences from behind and took a radio station and a barracks.</li><li><b>Marines land in the rear and the line is broken</b> HMS Anthony ran past the harbour defences and landed fifty Royal Marines among the Vichy rear. The 17th Brigade broke through the defences. The South Lancashires withdrew after their radio failed.</li><li><b>Antisarane surrenders</b> With the Vichy defence broken, Antisarane surrendered that evening, though substantial Vichy forces withdrew to the south.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranavalona-iii.jpg" alt="Ranavalona III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-iii">Ranavalona III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ranavalona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">J. Geiser. Algier phot., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Diego-Suarez is a large bay with a fine harbour near the northern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, opening to the east through the narrow Oronjia Pass. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> coveted it in the 1880s as a coaling station for steamships, and after the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-hova-war">Franco-Hova War</a> Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-iii">Ranavalona III</a> signed a treaty on 17 December 1885 giving France a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the bay and the surrounding territory. In 1941 the town, the bay and the channel were well protected by naval shore batteries.</p><p>By the end of February 1942 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, and its submarines moved freely through the northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. In March Japanese carriers raided shipping in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> and attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trincomalee">Trincomalee</a>, which drove the British Eastern Fleet to a new base at Kilindini, Mombasa. Japanese Kaidai-type submarines had a range of more than 10,000 miles in some cases, so bases on Madagascar would have affected Allied communications from the Pacific to the South Atlantic. German officers pressed the Japanese to give Madagascar priority, and the Japanese would not disclose their plans for it.</p><p>On 27 November 1941 the British Chiefs of Staff discussed the possibility that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> government might cede Madagascar to Japan or let its navy use the island. On 16 December de Gaulle urged Churchill to act. Churchill did not want a joint British and Free French operation after the Battle of Dakar in 1940. By 12 March 1942 he was convinced, the Free French were explicitly excluded, and on 14 March Force 121 was constituted under Major-General Robert Sturges, with Rear-Admiral Edward Syfret in command at sea.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Madagascar&#8221; cites where it covers Geopolitical, Vichy, Allies, Allied preparations:</p><ul><li>Leonard Charles Frederick Turner, War in the Southern Oceans: 1939–1945 (Oxford University Press, 1961)</li><li>John Grehan, Churchill's Secret Invasion : Britain's First Large Scale Combined Offensive 1942 (Pen &amp; Sword Military, 2013)</li><li>Jon Sutherland, Vichy Air Force at War: The French Air Force that Fought the Allies in World War II (Pen &amp; Sword Aviation, 2011)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Force 121 left the Clyde on 23 March, met Syfret's ships at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> and assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a>, where the 13th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> Group of the 5th Division joined it. Syfret's squadron included the battleship HMS Ramillies, the carriers HMS Illustrious and HMS Indomitable, the cruisers Hermione and Devonshire, eleven destroyers, six minesweepers and six corvettes, over 50 vessels in all. The landing force was the 29th Independent Infantry Brigade Group, No. 5 Commando and two brigades of the 5th Infantry Division. The chiefs of staff were adamant that the operation must succeed, preferably without any fighting. It was the first British amphibious assault since the Dardanelles twenty-seven years before.</p><p>The defenders were led by Governor General Armand Léon Annet and numbered about 8,000 troops, about 6,000 of them Malagasy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirailleurs">tirailleurs</a>, a large proportion of the rest being Senegalese. Between 1,500 and 3,000 were concentrated around Diego-Suarez. Naval and air defences were relatively light or obsolete: eight coastal batteries, two armed merchant cruisers, two sloops, five submarines, 17 M.S. 406 fighters and ten Potez 63 bombers.</p><p>Field-Marshal Smuts urged that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahajanga">Majunga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toamasina">Tamatave</a> be occupied as well, but Diego-Suarez was kept as the only objective for lack of manpower. Antisarane, the naval base, was elaborately defended with trenches, two redoubts and pillboxes, and flanked by impenetrable swamps. The British were unaware of the strength of these defences, known as the Joffre line.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 5 May, after reconnaissance flights by the South African Air Force, the 29th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> and No. 5 Commando went ashore in landing craft at Courrier Bay and Ambararata Bay, west of Diego-Suarez, while a diversionary attack was staged to the east. Swordfish and Albacore aircraft from the carriers attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> shipping and Arrachart airfield and sank the armed merchant cruiser Bougainville and the submarine Bévéziers. The landings met virtually no resistance. The 17th Brigade took the town of Diego-Suarez after toiling through mangrove swamp and bush, while the 29th Brigade advanced 21 miles towards Antisarane with a few tanks.</p><p>On the morning of 6 May a frontal assault on the Joffre line failed, and by the end of the day 10 of the 12 tanks had been destroyed. The South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashires</a> worked round the defences and caused chaos behind them, taking a radio station and a barracks, but withdrew when their radio failed. Admiral Darlan told Annet to defend the honour of the flag and to make the British pay dearly.</p><p>The deadlock was broken when the old destroyer HMS Anthony dashed past the harbour defences and landed fifty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-marines">Royal Marines</a> in the rear, who took the artillery command post, a barracks and the naval depot. The 17th Brigade broke through and Antisarane surrendered that evening, though substantial Vichy forces withdrew to the south. Operation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ironclad">Ironclad</a> ended on 7 May.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In three days the British had 109 men killed and 283 wounded and the French about 700 casualties; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> later gave about 500 casualties for the landing at Diego-Suarez. By 7 May twelve Morane fighters and five Potez 63s had been destroyed of 35 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> aircraft on the island. On 29 May Japanese submarines arrived, and a midget submarine damaged Ramillies and sank the tanker British Loyalty, later refloated. The crew of the other, Lieutenant Saburo Akieda and Petty Officer Masami Takemoto, were betrayed in the village of Anijabe and killed in a firefight with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-marines">Royal Marines</a>.</p><p>Fighting then fell to a low level. Mayotte was taken on 2 July without loss of life. The campaign to secure the rest of the island opened with landings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahajanga">Majunga</a> on 10 September and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toamasina">Tamatave</a> on 18 September, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Tananarive</a> fell on 23 September. A landing at Tulear followed on 29 September. The last major action was the ambush at Andramanalina on 18 October, where 800 Vichy troops surrendered. Annet, who had escaped, asked for terms; an armistice was signed at Ambalavao on 6 November and he surrendered two days later. The fighting after 10 September cost the Allies 30 killed and 90 wounded.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The ships, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> and squadrons on each side are known by name, and Churchill's guidelines to the planners of Operation Bonus are recorded. The dead and wounded of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ironclad">Ironclad</a> are put at 109 killed and 283 wounded for the British, and about 700 casualties for the French; a figure of about 500 casualties for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in the landing at Diego-Suarez also stands. Darlan's message to Annet is recorded in his own words.</p><p>The weight of the campaign is a matter of judgement. Julian Jackson, in his biography of de Gaulle, observed that the French held out longer against the Allies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> in 1942 than they had against the Germans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1940. John Grehan has claimed that the capture of the island before it could fall into Japanese hands was so crucial that it led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s eventual downfall and defeat. The Japanese refused to disclose their plans for Madagascar to their German partners, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a>'s request to Japan for help found Japan in no position to give substantial support.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> in Allied hands, military and naval installations were established across the island. Its deep-water ports were vital for control of the passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the Persian corridor and were now beyond the reach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>. In the makeshift Allied planning of the early years of the war the invasion held a prominent strategic place, and it was the first large-scale Allied operation to combine sea, land and air forces.</p><p>The island was placed under Free French control. General Paul Legentilhomme was appointed High Commissioner for Madagascar in December 1942, only to replace British administration. Like many colonies, Madagascar sought independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> after the war. In 1947 the island experienced the Malagasy Uprising, a costly revolution that was crushed in 1948, and on 26 June 1960 the Malagasy Republic proclaimed its independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about twelve years later.</p><p>Campaign service in Madagascar did not qualify for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> Star; it was covered by the 1939–1945 Star.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>From 5 May 1942 British and Allied forces took Diego-Suarez and then the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, to keep its ports from the Japanese navy. About 6,000 of the 8,000 defenders were Malagasy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirailleurs">tirailleurs</a>, and an armistice was signed on 6 November.</em></p>

